security and privacy documentation for the system is vital to assessing and determining risk


the security and privacy plans are the basis for establishing the requirements, categorization of data, controls, and procedures that will protect the system and its data. 


planning documents define the security and privacy objectives, scope, boundaries, roles, responsibilities, and controls for the system. implementation documents describe how the security and privacy controls are implemented, tested, monitored, and maintained for the system


within the NIST 800-37 risk management framework (RMF), the system security and privacy plan (SSP) is used as both the planning and implementation documentation

this document is used to plan and implement security and privacy controls; then, it is updates with the procedures and guidelines used to verify implementation. ISO 270001 has a similar document containing the requirement for the statement of applicability (SoA). these documents are key to evaluating system compliance. there are both similarities and differences in the system compliance authorization process between ISO 27001 and NIST 800-37. the following table provides a summary of the scope, approach, documents, roles, and benefits of each


ISO 27001: scope; covers the entire ISMS of the organization, which may include multiple information systems and processes. focuses on certification, offering a formal recognition of compliance. approach; based on the plan, do, check, act (PDCA) cycle, which is a continuous improvement model. takes a broader approach to information security management, requiring organizations to identify and treat risks in line with their own context and objectives. documents; requires eight types of documents for the ISMS, which are mainly focused on the risk assessment and treatment process and the security controls selection and implementation. roles; involves the top management, the ISMS team, and internal auditors, and the external certification body. benefits; provides a comprehensive and holistic framework for managing the security and privacy of the organizations information assets and demonstrates the organizations commitment and compliance to the international standard. catered to organizations seeking a broad and flexible information security management system. 

NIST SP 800-37: scope; covers a single information system or a group of interconnected information systems within the organization. focuses on compliance within the context of U.S. federal requirements without a certification process. approach; based on the risk management framework (RMF), which is a risk management model. provides a step by step risk management framework more closely tier to the system development process. documents; requires seven types of documents for the RMF, which are mainly focused on the security and privacy control assessment and authorization process and the continuous monitoring strategy. roles; involves the system owner, the system security officer, the security and privacy control assessor, and the authorizing official. benefits; provides a structured and consistent framework for managing the security and privacy risk of the organizations information systems and supports the organizations decision making and accountability to the federal guidelines. catered to organizations seeking a structured, technology focused framework


