System Risk Acceptance Criteria



reviewing the system security posture requires and understanding of the system risk acceptance criteria. the system risk acceptance criteria provide the acceptable level of risk to the organization


the risk acceptance criteria are tied directly to the organizational risk management strategy and the organizations defined risk appetite. reviewing a system for compliance within the risk acceptance criteria includes analyzing the likelihood and impact of risk events on the system and information. this risk can be evaluated utilizing qualitative and quantitative risk analysis methods

each security and privacy control may have a different risk acceptance criterion. a GRC professional determines the acceptable level of risk by comparing it to the organizational risk tolerance. this tolerance has different context, objectives, and requirements depending on how the system and information are categorized


NIST 800-37 and ISO 27001 provide guidance to risk management but have different approaches to measuring risk

ISO utilizes a systematic approach within the information security and management system (ISMS) to ensure protection of information assets. 

NIST 800-37 uses the authorization phase of the risk management framework (RMF) to assess and analyze risk based on the authorization package submitted by the system owner. this authorization package is analyzed against risk assessment documentation and the organizational risk management strategy.


within both ISO and NIST, risk analysis and risk acceptance criteria are handled with the following steps: identify system risk sources, events, scenarios, and factors that can affect the system and its objective. analyses system risk in terms of the likelihood and impact of system risk events and the effectiveness and efficiency of system risk controls. evaluate system risk and compare it with the system risk acceptance criteria to determine the risk level and need for treatment. treat system risk by selecting and implementing appropriate system risk controls to mitigate, avoid, transfer, or accept system risk. monitor and review system risk and system risk controls regularly to ensure their adequacy, relevancy, suitability, and performance in the face of evolving threats, technological advancement and changing business environments. communicate and consult with relevant stakeholders throughout the system risk management process to ensure their awareness, involvement, and support.


