Stakeholder Concurrence for Risk Treatment Options



stakeholder concurrence is the level of agreement and alignment that stakeholders of the system have on the objectives, requirements, and risks of the system.


these stakeholders include individuals and groups that influence the system and the information on that system (e.g. users, data owners, system owners, developers, managers). stakeholders can also include the customers and regulators who interact with and enforce regulatory compliance, respectively

maintaining and understand the perspective of these stakeholders provides accuracy in the level of risk associated with systems. understanding the risks associated with these stakeholders ensures system compliance is acceptable and manageable to these risks


this involves obtaining agreement from all relevant stakeholder on the chosen methods to address identified risks, ensuring that risk treatment plans are not only technically sound but also aligned with organizational objectives, stakeholder expectations, and available resources. stakeholders have influence on the risk treatment as well. here are some ways that stakeholder concurrence may influence risk treatment:

stakeholder concurrence helps identify and prioritize the risks that must be treated based on the potential impact of the risk to the organization. stakeholder concurrence helps select and implement the most appropriate and feasible risk treatment options based on the stakeholders input and feedback. stakeholder concurrence helps monitor and evaluate the effectiveness and outcomes of the risk treatment options based on the stakeholders expectations and satisfaction.

its important to consider the various stakeholder and their influence on system risk and compliance. customers, as external stakeholders, can also influence the organizational strategy toward risk. regulators and auditors who influence penalties and fines also influence the treatment of risk by the organization. the organizational risk management strategy and risk tolerance is the primary guide for treatment of risk and overall system compliance. once the risk treatment of the system and information on the system are determined to be compliant and acceptable within the organizations risk tolerance and appetite, that system can be authorized to operate in production.


