Risk Documentation Elements to Define Residual Risk



residual risk is important in risk management because it reflects the actual level of risk exposure an organization faces even after taking actions to address the risk


risk documentation should include the following elements to define residual risk: 

risk identification and analysis describes the sources, causes, and consequences of the risk, its likelihood, and its impact. the risk treatment outlines the implemented risk controls, mitigation strategies, or plans to reduce the risk. the residual risk rating assigns a value to the residual risk based on its likelihood and impact after treatment. the residual risk acceptance states whether the residual risk is within the risk appetite or tolerance of the organization or further treatment is required. the residual risk monitoring and review specifies how the residual risk will be tracked, measures, and reported over time, and how the risk treatment will be evaluated for its effectiveness and efficiency. 

residual risk helps determine whether the treatment is adequate or that additional measures are needed to further reduce the risk. understanding residual risk will also help GRC professionals make decisions, allocate resources, and communicate the risk status and performance to stakeholders. residual risk can indicate the potential opportunities or benefits that may arise from accepting a certain level of risk. once the residual risk has been addressed and determined to be at an acceptable risk tolerance level within the organizations risk appetite, the risk determination should be formally documented and communicated as part of the system compliance documentation.


